        "reasoning_content": "Here's a thinking process:\n\n1.  **Analyze User Input:**\n    *   **Gap ID:** `Nce5ee7f3445e`\n    *   **Intended Use:** `mechanism`\n    *   **Question:** \"What standardized analytical protocols (LC-MS/MS or GC-MS parameters, sample processing standards) exist for quantifying absolute physiological concentrations of inosine and short-chain fatty acids in human serum or tumor tissue, and are there cancer-specific reference ranges or consensus statements?\"\n    *   **User Scope:** \"How does the gut microbiome modulate the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumours, and what mechanisms mediate that effect?\"\n    *   **Material Found:** 3 papers (P0642, P0639, P0638). All are methodological/validation papers focusing on metabolite quantification.\n        *   P0642 (2022): GC-MS protocol for SCFAs in plasma, serum, feces, mouse cecum. Uses surrogate matrix (water). Validates linearity, precision, recovery, parallelism. Provides healthy control reference ranges for SCFAs in serum. Does *not* cover cancer patients or tumor tissue. Does *not* cover inosine.\n        *   P0639 (2023): LC-MS/MS protocol for nucleosides/deoxynucleosides (including inosine) in *urine* from bladder cancer patients. Uses LMM for longitudinal data. Reports pre/post-surgery inosine levels normalized to creatinine. Does *not* cover serum or tumor tissue. Does *not* cover SCFAs.\n        *   P0638 (2016): LC-MS/MS protocol for adenosine, guanosine, inosine *nucleotides* (mono/triphosphates) in PBMCs, RBCs, DBS. Focuses on intracellular nucleotides, not free inosine in serum/tissue. Highlights stability issues and contamination risks. Does *not* cover serum/tumor free inosine or SCFAs.\n    *   **Paper Context:** Matches sections confirm these are method development/validation papers. No direct clinical outcome data linking these metabolites to ICI efficacy is present in the provided text.\n    *   **Success Criteria:** Identify at least one peer-reviewed methodological paper or consensus guideline specifying: (1) analytical platform parameters for inosine or SCFA quantification in human serum/tumor samples, (2) sample processing standards, and (3) reference concentration ranges in cancer patients or healthy controls.\n\n2.  **Evaluate Materials against Question & Success Criteria:**\n    *   *Inosine in serum/tumor:* None of the papers provide a validated protocol for *free inosine* in human serum or tumor tissue. P0639 covers urine. P0638 covers intracellular nucleotides in blood cells.\n    *   *SCFAs in serum/tumor:* P0642 provides a GC-MS protocol for serum/plasma and mentions mouse cecum tissue, but explicitly states it does not cover human tumor tissue. It provides healthy control ranges, not cancer-specific ranges.\n    *   *Cancer-specific reference ranges/consensus statements:* Explicitly missing across all materials. P0639 gives post-op urine levels, not serum/tumor cancer ranges. P0642 gives healthy serum ranges. P0638 gives cellular nucleotide levels.\n    *   *Conclusion:* The materials do *not* fulfill the success criteria. They provide methodological insights for related matrices (urine, blood cells, healthy serum) and metabolites (SCFAs, nucleotides), but lack the specific target (free inosine in serum/tumor, SCFAs in tumor tissue) and cancer-specific reference ranges/consensus guidelines requested.\n\n3.  **Extract Quantitative Comparisons (if any):**\n    *   P0642 (SCFAs in serum/plasma): Acetic acid range: 4788–8823 ng/mL (healthy controls). LOD/LOQ for propionic/butyric/valeric: 12.67–28.06 (ng/mL or similar unit implied, text says \"ranged between 12.67–28.06\"). Inter-day precision RSD < 10%. Recovery 94–109%.\n    *   P0639 (Inosine in urine): LOQ 0.040 µM. Linearity 0.05–5 µM. Pre-surgery concentration: 0.16 ± 0.12 µM/mM creatinine.\n    *   P0638 (Inosine nucleotides in PBMC/RBC): Linearity 0.25–50 pmol/sample for ITP. Intra/interassay %CV 1.7–16%. Accuracy %deviation -11.5 to 14.7%. ATP/GTP/ITP in PBMCs vs RBCs: 10, 55, 5.6 fold higher respectively.\n    *   I will format these strictly as requested: `research_object`, `result_or_measure`, `setting_or_time`, `groups`.\n    *   Note: The prompt says \"一组一数成对，不用多组名字对应一串数字\". I'll list them carefully.\n\n    Let's draft the quantitative comparisons:\n    - research_object: SCFAs in human serum/plasma; result_or_measure: acetic acid concentration; setting_or_time: healthy controls; groups: [{group: \"serum\", value: \"4788–8823 ng/mL\"}]\n    - research_object: SCFAs in human serum/plasma; result_or_measure: LOQ for propionic, butyric, valeric acid; setting_or_time: method validation; groups: [{group: \"plasma/serum\", value: \"12.67–28.06 (unit unspecified in text, likely ng/mL or μg/mL based on context)\"}] -> I'll stick strictly to text: \"12.67–28.06\"\n    - research_object: Inosine in human urine; result_or_measure: LOQ; setting_or_time: method validation; groups: [{group: \"urine\", value: \"0.040 µM\"}]\n    - research_object: Inosine in human urine; result_or_measure: concentration; setting_or_time: pre-surgery; groups: [{group: \"bladder cancer patients\", value: \"0.16 ± 0.12 µM/mM creatinine\"}]\n    - research_object: Inosine triphosphate (ITP) in human blood cells; result_or_measure: linearity range; setting_or_time: method validation; groups: [{group: \"PBMC/RBC/DBS\", value: \"0.25–50 pmol/sample\"}]\n    - research_object: Nucleotides in human blood cells; result_or_measure: relative abundance ratio (PBMC vs RBC); setting_or_time: comparative analysis; groups: [{group: \"ATP\", value: \"10-fold\"}, {group: \"GTP\", value: \"55-fold\"}, {group: \"ITP\", value: \"5.6-fold\"}] -> Wait, prompt says \"一组一数成对\". I'll split them or keep as listed if they are grouped in text. Text says \"10, 55, and 5.6 fold for ATP, GTP, and ITP, respectively\". I'll list them separately to be safe.\n    Actually, I'll just list the clear ones matching the prompt's structure.\n\n    Let's refine quantitat